{"name":"Chrome","hex":"#e1dfe1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chrome","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Chromium, when used to plate other metals.","The basic structural elements used in a graphical user interface, such as window frames and scroll bars, as opposed to the content."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To plate with chrome.","To treat with a solution of potassium bichromate, as in dyeing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chrome"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chromium","blocks":[{"text":"Chromium is a chemical element; it has symbol Cr and atomic number 24. It is the first element in group 6. It is a steely-grey, lustrous, hard, and brittle transition metal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Chromium_crystals_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Chromium_crystals_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromium"},{"title":"Chrome plating","blocks":[{"text":"Chrome plating is a technique of electroplating a thin layer of chromium onto a metal object. A chrome plated part is called chrome, or is said to have been chromed. The chromium layer can be decorative, provide corrosion resistance, facilitate cleaning, and increase surface hardness. Sometimes a less expensive substitute for chrome, such as nickel, may be used for aesthetic purposes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Motorcycle_Reflections_bw_edit.jpg/330px-Motorcycle_Reflections_bw_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrome_plating"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Chromium.jpg/500px-Chromium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Chromium.jpg/500px-Chromium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org